## Why This Is Asked

This question explores advanced incremental data-structure design under streaming constraints, requiring mastery of amortized analysis for batched updates alongside exact substring query capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- **Suffix Automaton fundamentals**: States, transitions, links, and the endpos equivalence relation
- **Batched extension strategies**: Buffering B characters before committing to the global structure
- **Clone management and amortized cost**: Deferred clone creation and lazy propagation techniques
- **Transition representations**: Compact maps vs. dense arrays for cache locality
- **Trade-off analysis**: Balancing batch size B, alphabet size σ, and total string length N

## Code Example

```cpp
struct State {
    int link;
    int len;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> next;  // or vector<int> for small σ
};

class BatchedSuffixAutomaton {
    std::vector<State> st;
    int last;
    std::string buffer;
    const size_t B;

public:
    BatchedSuffixAutomaton(size_t batch_size = 1024) : B(batch_size), last(0) {
        st.push_back({-1, 0, {}});
    }

    void extend(char c) {
        buffer.push_back(c);
        if (buffer.size() < B) return;
        flushBuffer();
    }

    void flushBuffer() {
        for (char c : buffer) {
            int cur = st.size();
            st.push_back({0, st[last].len + 1, {}});
            int p = last;
            while (p >= 0 && !st[p].next.count(c)) {
                st[p].next[c] = cur;
                p = st[p].link;
            }
            if (p == -1) {
                st[cur].link = 0;
            } else {
                int q = st[p].next[c];
                if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                    st[cur].link = q;
                } else {
                    int clone = st.size();
                    st.push_back(st[q]);  // clone
                    st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                    while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                        st[p].next[c] = clone;
                        p = st[p].link;
                    }
                    st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
                }
            }
            last = cur;
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    bool query(const std::string& p) {
        int v = 0;
        for (char c : p) {
            if (!st[v].next.count(c)) return false;
            v = st[v].next[c];
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

## Optimal Batch Size

The optimal B depends on workload characteristics:
- **Small σ (e.g., DNA, σ ≤ 4)**: Larger B (4096+) works well; dense transition arrays gain from cache locality
- **Large σ (e.g., Unicode, σ ≈ 10⁵)**: Smaller B (128–512); sparse maps reduce memory pressure
- **Memory-constrained environments**: B ≈ √N balances update frequency against per-batch overhead
- **Latency-sensitive queries**: Smaller B reduces worst-case query delay between flushes

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a practical data structure solution for maintaining top-K items under batch updates, balancing per-update cost and output cost, and comparing against naive approaches.

## Key Concepts
- **Top-K maintenance** with dynamic count updates
- **Batch amortization** — distributing work across batch items rather than per-event
- **Heap with lazy updates** — using a min-heap for efficient top-K tracking
- **Space-time trade-offs** — comparing near-linear space with output efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
class TopKTracker {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap(); // stores (count, id) pairs
  }

  processBatch(updates) {
    // Phase 1: Apply all deltas to hashmap
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const newCount = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + delta;
      this.counts.set(id, newCount);
    }

    // Phase 2: Reconcile heap with updated counts
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const count = this.counts.get(id);
      if (this.heap.has(id)) {
        this.heap.update(id, count);
      } else if (this.heap.size < this.k) {
        this.heap.insert(id, count);
      } else if (count > this.heap.peekMin().count) {
        this.heap.replaceMin(id, count);
      }
    }
  }

  getTopK() {
    return this.heap.getAllSorted(); // returns K items sorted by count descending
  }
}
```

## Amortized Analysis
Each batch processes B events. Updating the hashmap costs O(B) total. Each heap operation costs O(log K), and at most B such operations occur per batch, yielding O(B log K) amortized time. Since B << N in practice (batch sizes are typically much smaller than total unique keys), this significantly outperforms re-scanning all N items after every batch, which costs O(N log N) per batch.

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical online order-statistics maintenance with batched updates and coordinate compression.

## Key Concepts
